problems.problem:
Six students took part in a meeting. It is known that:

i) in any group of three students, at least two are friends;

ii) there are seven pairs of friends.

Prove that among the six students:

a) there is a student who has at least three friends;

b) there are at least three students who are friends with one another.

The real numbers $a$ and $b$ have the properties:

i) $0 < a < b$;

ii) $b - a \ge \dfrac{1}{2}$;

iii) $a^{40} + b^{40} = 1$.

Prove that in the decimal representation of $b$, the first $12$ digits after the decimal point are equal to $9$.

In the rectangular parallelepiped $ABCDA'B'C'D'$ denote by $M$ the centre of the face $ABB'A'$. Denote by $M_1$ and $M_2$ the projections of $M$ onto the lines $B'C$ and $AD'$, respectively. Prove that:

a) $[MM_1] \equiv [MM_2]$;

b) if $(MM_1M_2) \cap (ABC) = d$, then $d \parallel AD$;

c) $m\big(\angle((MM_1M_2), (ABC))\big) = 45^\circ \iff \dfrac{BC}{AB} = \dfrac{BB'}{BC} + \dfrac{BC}{BB'}$.

Prove that:

a) there exist infinitely many pairs $(x, y)$ of real numbers from the interval $\left[0, \sqrt{3}\right]$ which satisfy the equality $x \cdot \sqrt{3 - y^2} + y \cdot \sqrt{3 - x^2} = 3$;

b) there is no pair $(x, y)$ of rational numbers from the interval $\left[0, \sqrt{3}\right]$ for which the equality $x \cdot \sqrt{3 - y^2} + y \cdot \sqrt{3 - x^2} = 3$ holds.
